-- allium: 3
-- memory.allium
--
-- NanoClaw v2 memory indexing and semantic search.
-- Each agent group has an embedded memory index built from markdown and org files in its workspace
-- (groups/<group>/memory/, the group's Zettelkasten at groups/<group>/zettel/, and the Zotero
-- library's item files at groups/<group>/zotero-md/ in the group it syncs into), plus any of
-- the group's additional mounts marked `index` (e.g. an org-mode tree). Agents can
-- search memory using natural language queries; results use
-- hybrid vector + keyword scoring.
--
-- Scope: memory indexing and retrieval
-- Includes: file watching, chunking, embedding, hybrid search, FTS fallback
-- Excludes:
--   - Core routing (see routing.allium, sessions.allium)
--   - Embedding provider internals (treated as black box)
--   - sqlite-vec extension mechanics (implementation detail)
--
-- Ported from v1; implemented in src/memory/ (host) and container/agent-runner/src/mcp-tools/memory.ts.
-- Key v2 differences from v1:
--   - Groups are now AgentGroups (identity.allium); the indexed roots are groups/<folder>/memory/
--     and groups/<folder>/zettel/ (the Zettelkasten, kept apart from the memory/ tree because its
--     notes are permanent where memory/ is updated and pruned in place; see memory-graph.allium),
--     plus workspace folders another module registers for the groups it writes into — today
--     groups/<folder>/zotero-md/, the Zotero library (zotero.allium), indexed as source "zotero"
--     in the group named by its sync config only; and the group's additional mounts marked
--     `index` in its container config, stored under the path the container sees them at,
--     extra/<mount>/… (source = the mount's name), so a hit maps straight to
--     /workspace/extra/<mount>/… whatever the host layout
--   - MemoryFile and MemoryChunk are realised by the group's own index
--     (data/v2-memory/<group>/index.db: its files and chunks tables, with the keyword and
--     vector rows for each file written in the same transaction). There is no central copy.
--     A file is indexed when it has a row there; pending means the watcher or a sync has
--     seen it change and it is not yet (re)written — held by the host in memory, not stored,
--     so a restart re-derives it by comparing content hashes.
--   - The v1 IPC query path is replaced by the MemoryMcpTools surface (see bottom of file);
--     MCP tools are registered in container/agent-runner/src/mcp-tools/. Search is still
--     fulfilled by the host — it holds the embedding credential and the embedding budget —
--     over the session DBs: the container writes a memory_search_request system message to
--     outbound.db and waits for the matching response in inbound.db (the same envelope as
--     ncl's cli_request). Get and list read the group's read-only index mount directly.
--   - Write path differs by agent type (see MemoryFilesWithinWorkspace invariant comment):
--       non-specialist agents — group dir mounted RW; agents write to memory/ directly
--       specialist agents     — group dir mounted RO; files land via commit_to_memory on result delivery
--
-- Specialist exclusion: memory indexing, watching and search are disabled for specialist
-- agent groups (groups with an associated identity/Specialist row — see identity.allium).
-- The host does not begin indexing any group until every module that excludes groups
-- from memory has loaded what it excludes, so a specialist group is never indexed
-- because the host started before it knew the group was a specialist.
-- A specialist group has no index of its own: nothing in its folder is watched or indexed.
-- Read access to memory is by the session, not the group: memory_group_for(session)
-- names the one group whose index a session may search, get and list -
--   a non-specialist session      its own group;
--   a memory-provider task session the root requester group of its task chain (the
--                                  main group the chain serves), read-only;
--   any other specialist session   none - no memory tools, no index mount.
-- A specialist that needs memory dispatches a memory-provider specialist as a sub-task
-- (specialists.allium MemoryProviderDispatched) and gets its answer as the result. The
-- researcher in particular never reaches memory (memory-graph.allium
-- ResearcherKnowledgeIsolation). The rules here gate on that session-to-group mapping,
-- and indexing on `group.specialist = null`.
--
-- On/off switch: memory as a whole is active only when an embedding API key is configured
-- (config.embedding_key_configured). There is no separate enable flag: without a key the
-- host runs no index for any group — nothing is watched or indexed, and no group's
-- containers get the memory MCP tools or the index mount.
--
-- Group allowlist: memory is only active for agent groups whose folder name appears in
-- config.memory_search_groups (default: { "main" }). Groups not in the allowlist are
-- silently inactive — no MemoryFile is created and no MCP tools are available to them.
--
-- Embedding budget: the embedding limits (config.embedding_rpm_limit, embedding_tpm_limit,
-- embedding_rpd_budget) belong to the upstream account — one key, one upstream — not to a
-- group. Every indexed group's indexing and every search's query embedding draw on the same
-- shared budget, and a daily stop met by one group stops embedding for all of them.

use "./identity.allium" as identity
use "./sessions.allium" as sessions

------------------------------------------------------------
-- Value Types
------------------------------------------------------------

-- A scored search result returned to the querying agent: one file, with the places
-- in it that matched. Return type of the opaque memory_hybrid_search black-box function
-- (see the Host-Side Helpers comment block below). Allium does not support
-- module-qualified references to free functions, so this value type cannot be formally
-- referenced from rules even though MemorySearched binds its results.
-- allium-ignore allium.definition.unused
value MemorySearchResult {
    file: MemoryFile
    score: Decimal                      -- the best of its locations' scores
    locations: List<MemorySearchLocation> -- best first, at most config.search_locations_per_file
    more_locations: Integer             -- matching locations left out by that cap
}

-- One matching place in a result's file: a chunk, or overlapping chunks merged.
value MemorySearchLocation {
    start_line: Integer
    end_line: Integer
    score: Decimal                      -- 0.0 – 1.0; hybrid of vector and keyword scores
    snippet: String                     -- excerpt; full length for the best location, shorter for the rest
}

-- Caller-supplied parameters for memory_hybrid_search.
-- All fields are optional; the host fills in defaults from config when absent.
value MemorySearchParams {
    limit: Integer?                     -- cap on results returned (defaults to config.search_top_k)
    min_score: Decimal?                 -- minimum score (defaults by mode: config.min_search_score hybrid,
                                        -- config.min_keyword_score keyword-only)
    path_prefix: String?                -- restrict search to files under this path prefix
    source: String?                     -- restrict search to files from a named source
    include_content: Boolean?           -- whether to include full chunk content alongside snippets
    include_superseded: Boolean?        -- include superseded notes (default false: current knowledge only)
}

-- Caller-supplied parameters for memory_list_files.
-- All fields are optional; the host fills in defaults when absent.
value MemoryListParams {
    path_prefix: String?                -- restrict listing to files under this path prefix
    source: String?                     -- restrict listing to files from a named source
    limit: Integer?                     -- cap on files returned
    order_by: String?                   -- mtime | path | size
    parse_frontmatter: Boolean?         -- whether to parse and include YAML frontmatter (limit ≤ 50 when true)
}

------------------------------------------------------------
-- Host-Side Helpers
------------------------------------------------------------

-- The following four black-box functions are implemented by the host process and
-- exposed to agents as MCP tool calls (see container/agent-runner/src/mcp-tools/).
-- They are referenced by bare (unqualified) names because Allium does not support
-- module-qualified references to free functions; the memory_ prefix marks the module
-- boundary instead.
--
--   memory_hybrid_search(group: identity/AgentGroup, query: String, params: MemorySearchParams)
--     → list of MemorySearchResult
--     Combines vector similarity (sqlite-vec cosine distance) and FTS5 keyword match,
--     weighted by config.vector_score_weight and config.keyword_score_weight. When the
--     vector index is unavailable (sqlite-vec not loaded) the vector half is an in-process
--     cosine scan over the stored chunk embeddings — still hybrid. Keyword-only applies
--     when the query itself cannot be embedded (see MemorySearched); its results are
--     scored by keyword match alone, unweighted, and a null params.min_score means
--     config.min_keyword_score there (config.min_search_score when hybrid).
--
--   memory_get_file_content(group: identity/AgentGroup, path: String, parse_frontmatter: Boolean)
--     → file content (optionally with parsed frontmatter metadata)
--     Reads a single indexed memory file by path. Returns the raw content plus
--     optional frontmatter fields when parse_frontmatter = true.
--
--   memory_list_files(group: identity/AgentGroup, params: MemoryListParams)
--     → list of indexed files matching the filter
--     Enumerates MemoryFile records for the group, applying path_prefix, source,
--     order_by, and limit filters from the params.
--
--   chunk_file(path: String, chunk_tokens: Integer, overlap_tokens: Integer)
--     → list of (start_line: Integer, end_line: Integer, content: String, hash: String)
--     Reads the file at path and splits its content into overlapping chunks for embedding.
--     Dispatches to a format-specific chunker based on file extension:
--       .md  — line-by-line, trailing-line overlap (chunkMarkdown)
--       .org — heading-boundary splits with parent-heading context carried into child
--              sections instead of character overlap (chunkOrgMode)
--     Token counting uses a 4-chars-per-token character approximation (no real tokenizer).
--     chunk_tokens and overlap_tokens map to chunk_tokens*4 and overlap_tokens*4 characters.
--     Returns [] for empty files. Blocks and drawers in .org files are never split across
--     chunk boundaries even if they exceed the token target.
--     hash is SHA-256 of the chunk text; the key of the group's embedding cache (per model).
--
-- The memory_* helpers are read-only with respect to the memory index. chunk_file is
-- read-only with respect to the filesystem — it reads but does not mutate MemoryFile
-- or MemoryChunk entities.

------------------------------------------------------------
-- Entities
------------------------------------------------------------

-- A file in a group's workspace that is tracked and indexed for semantic search.
-- Files are re-indexed whenever their content changes, and all of them when the index's
-- settings change (IndexSettingsChanged). A file that leaves the workspace leaves the index:
-- its record is deleted, not kept as a tombstone, so a file later created at the same path
-- is discovered afresh.
entity MemoryFile {
    group: identity/AgentGroup
    path: String                        -- groups/<folder>/zettel/notes/foo.md, or extra/<mount>/… for an indexed mount
    content_hash: String
    indexed_at: Timestamp?              -- null until first successful index
    -- True when the file is a Zettelkasten note whose frontmatter names a
    -- superseded_by (memory-graph.allium): read from the file when it is indexed,
    -- like its chunks. Search leaves superseded notes out unless asked for them.
    superseded: Boolean
    -- The triggering rules fire on WorkspaceFileChanged and WorkspaceFileRemoved — file-system
    -- watcher events emitted by the host process with no governing allium spec. They will always
    -- be external triggers. The checker cascades unreachable-trigger → unreachable-rule →
    -- unobserved status assignment. Lift if the checker suppresses that cascade for external triggers.
    -- allium-ignore allium.status.unreachableValue, allium.status.noExit
    status: pending | indexed

    -- Relationships
    chunks: MemoryChunk with file = this

    transitions status {
        pending -> indexed
        indexed -> pending              -- file changed, or the index settings changed; needs re-index
    }
}

-- A contiguous chunk of a memory file, stored with its embedding for vector search.
-- Chunks overlap slightly to preserve context across boundaries.
entity MemoryChunk {
    file: MemoryFile
    start_line: Integer
    end_line: Integer
    content: String
    hash: String                        -- SHA-256 of the chunk text; key of the group's embedding cache (per model)
    indexed_at: Timestamp
}

-- A group's index as a whole: the settings its current contents were built with.
-- settings is an opaque fingerprint of everything an unchanged file's entries depend on
-- besides its content — embedding provider and endpoint, model, vector dimensions, chunk
-- size and overlap, chunker version. Recorded only when a full pass reached every file.
entity MemoryIndex {
    group: identity/AgentGroup
    built_with: String?                 -- null until the first full pass completes
}

------------------------------------------------------------
-- Config
------------------------------------------------------------

config {
    chunk_tokens: Integer = 400
    chunk_overlap_tokens: Integer = 80
    search_top_k: Integer = 6                -- files per search
    search_max_top_k: Integer = 20           -- upper bound on a requested top_k; a larger one is capped
    search_locations_per_file: Integer = 3
    min_search_score: Decimal = 0.35
    min_keyword_score: Decimal = 0.1             -- floor for keyword-only results, scored by keyword alone
    search_host_timeout: Duration = 15.seconds   -- container waits this long for the host's answer
    search_embed_wait: Duration = 3.seconds      -- host waits this long for an embedding permit before keyword-only
    vector_score_weight: Decimal = 0.7
    keyword_score_weight: Decimal = 0.3
    embedding_provider: String = "gemini"
    -- The three embedding limits are per upstream account, shared by every indexed group.
    embedding_rpm_limit: Integer = 50   -- requests per minute to embedding provider
    embedding_tpm_limit: Integer = 15_000
    embedding_rpd_budget: Integer = 900 -- requests per 24h window; exhausted → pause 24h, then resume
    embedding_max_consecutive_refusals: Integer = 3 -- consecutive non-daily 429s that end a sync pass
    periodic_sync_interval: Duration = 24.hours     -- a full sync runs this often without any trigger
    embedding_key_configured: Boolean = false -- an embedding API key is set; the only on/off switch for memory.
                                              -- An operator secret, so a fresh install has none: memory is off
                                              -- until one is set
    memory_search_groups: Set<String> = { "main" } -- folder names of groups with memory active; comma-separated in env
}

------------------------------------------------------------
-- Rules
------------------------------------------------------------

-- ---- File Watching ----

-- A known file has changed content and needs re-indexing.
-- Covers both the indexed→pending and pending→pending (hash changed again before sync) cases.
-- The hash guard skips no-op watcher events (e.g. touch without content change) so the
-- embedding API is not called unnecessarily.
rule FileUpdated {
    when: WorkspaceFileChanged(group, path, content_hash)

    let file = MemoryFile{group: group, path: path}

    requires: group.specialist = null       -- memory indexing is disabled for specialist groups
    requires: group.folder in config.memory_search_groups  -- memory inactive for non-allowlisted groups
    requires: config.embedding_key_configured     -- no embedding key, no memory
    requires: exists file
    requires: content_hash != file.content_hash

    ensures: file.content_hash = content_hash
    ensures: file.status = pending
}

-- A new file has appeared in the workspace and needs initial indexing.
rule FileDiscovered {
    when: WorkspaceFileChanged(group, path, content_hash)

    requires: group.specialist = null       -- memory indexing is disabled for specialist groups
    requires: group.folder in config.memory_search_groups  -- memory inactive for non-allowlisted groups
    requires: config.embedding_key_configured     -- no embedding key, no memory
    requires: not exists MemoryFile{group: group, path: path}

    ensures: MemoryFile.created(
        group: group,
        path: path,
        content_hash: content_hash,
        status: pending,
        superseded: false
    )

    @guidance
        -- Includes a file created at the path of one removed earlier: FileRemoved deleted
        -- that record, so nothing of the old file stands in the way.
        -- Includes the files of a registered workspace folder (zettel/, zotero-md/) that did
        -- not exist when the host began indexing the group: every sync looks for such
        -- folders again — and every search starts one, without waiting for it — so a
        -- folder created later is watched and indexed from the next sync on, without a
        -- restart. A file created just before a search is found by the search after.
        -- Also fires when a specialist delivers a result with commit_to_memory = true
        -- (see specialists/AgentRequestsDeliverSpecialistResult). The host copies the
        -- file into the group's zettel/reports/ before WorkspaceFileChanged is emitted,
        -- so this rule fires under the same conditions as any other new file.
}

-- When a file is removed from the workspace, it and its chunks leave the index.
rule FileRemoved {
    when: WorkspaceFileRemoved(group, path)

    let file = MemoryFile{group: group, path: path}

    requires: group.specialist = null       -- memory indexing is disabled for specialist groups
    requires: group.folder in config.memory_search_groups  -- memory inactive for non-allowlisted groups
    requires: config.embedding_key_configured     -- no embedding key, no memory
    requires: exists file

    ensures:
        for chunk in file.chunks:
            not exists chunk
    ensures: not exists file

    @guidance
        -- FTS index rows and embedding rows for removed chunks are also deleted
        -- in the same transaction, so the search index never holds a vector or keyword row
        -- for a chunk that is gone, nor a chunk without its vector and keyword rows.
        -- WorkspaceFileRemoved is emitted from two sources: the filesystem watcher
        -- and the periodic sync sweep, which reconciles all tracked paths and emits
        -- the trigger for any file that has disappeared without a watcher event.
        -- The rule applies equally in both cases — the trigger source does not affect
        -- the postcondition.
}

-- ---- Indexing ----

-- A pending file is chunked, embedded, and its chunks stored for search.
-- Existing chunks for the file are replaced with the new set.
rule FileSynced {
    when: file: MemoryFile.status becomes pending

    requires: file.group.specialist = null  -- memory indexing is disabled for specialist groups
    requires: file.group.folder in config.memory_search_groups  -- memory inactive for non-allowlisted groups
    requires: config.embedding_key_configured     -- no embedding key, no memory
    requires: file.status = pending

    let new_chunks = chunk_file(file.path, config.chunk_tokens, config.chunk_overlap_tokens)

    ensures: file.status = indexed
    ensures: file.indexed_at = now
    ensures: file.superseded = names_superseded_by(file.path)
    ensures:
        for chunk in file.chunks:
            not exists chunk
    ensures:
        for c in new_chunks:
            MemoryChunk.created(
                file: file,
                start_line: c.start_line,
                end_line: c.end_line,
                content: c.content,
                hash: c.hash,
                indexed_at: now
            )

    @guidance
        -- Embeddings are generated in batches via the Gemini API with three rate limits:
        --   RPM  (requests per minute)   — causes in-session waiting; transparent to this rule
        --   TPM  (tokens per minute)     — causes in-session waiting; transparent to this rule
        --   RPD  (requests per day)      — aborts the sync; see below
        -- All three are global: one budget per upstream account (one key), shared by every
        -- indexed group's syncs and by search query embeddings, never one per group.
        -- A refusal (429) that is not the daily quota skips the file it was embedding for
        -- this pass: the file stays pending and the pass moves on to the next file. When
        -- config.embedding_max_consecutive_refusals such refusals come in a row, the pass
        -- ends as interrupted; every file not yet indexed stays pending until the next sync
        -- is triggered — a watcher event, a search (which runs a sync), or the periodic sync
        -- (config.periodic_sync_interval). An interrupted pass records no settings
        -- (IndexBuilt).
        -- The RPD budget (config.embedding_rpd_budget) is tracked in memory and counted over
        -- a 24h window opened by the first request it counts. When it is exhausted — or when
        -- the Gemini API returns a 429 with quotaType "rpd" — the host aborts the current sync
        -- immediately and stops embedding for 24h from that moment; a refusal met while the
        -- stop is in force does not extend it. Any files not yet processed remain pending.
        -- The host sets an internal dirty flag and schedules a sync() for when the stop lifts,
        -- so the pending files are retried without a process restart or a watcher event (a
        -- restart also retries them, since the stop is not persisted). No status change is
        -- needed: pending already means "not yet indexed, will be retried." RPM/TPM cool-down
        -- is handled transparently inside acquirePermit.
        -- Embeddings are cached in the group's index by chunk hash and model, so a
        -- repeated sync after budget recovery only embeds chunks whose hash is not yet cached.
}

-- ---- Index settings ----

-- The host opens a group's index for the first time: it starts with no recorded settings.
-- Its files arrive through FileDiscovered; the first full pass to reach them all records
-- the settings (IndexBuilt).
rule MemoryIndexCreated {
    when: MemoryIndexOpened(group, settings)

    requires: group.specialist = null       -- memory indexing is disabled for specialist groups
    requires: group.folder in config.memory_search_groups  -- memory inactive for non-allowlisted groups
    requires: config.embedding_key_configured     -- no embedding key, no memory
    requires: not exists MemoryIndex{group: group}

    ensures: MemoryIndex.created(group: group, built_with: null)
}

-- The host opens a group's index (at start) with settings that differ from those its
-- contents were built with: every file returns to pending, so each is chunked and embedded
-- again under the new settings. With unchanged settings this rule does not fire and only
-- files whose content changed are re-indexed (FileUpdated, FileDiscovered, FileRemoved).
rule IndexSettingsChanged {
    when: MemoryIndexOpened(group, settings)

    let index = MemoryIndex{group: group}

    requires: group.specialist = null       -- memory indexing is disabled for specialist groups
    requires: group.folder in config.memory_search_groups  -- memory inactive for non-allowlisted groups
    requires: config.embedding_key_configured     -- no embedding key, no memory
    requires: exists index
    requires: settings != index.built_with

    ensures:
        for f in MemoryFiles where f.group = group:
            f.status = pending

    @guidance
        -- Settings are read from configuration when the host starts, so a settings change
        -- takes effect at the next start; checking at open is equivalent to checking before
        -- every sync. The host logs each changed setting (old → new) and reports the
        -- re-index (reason, progress, outcome) on the dashboard. A full re-index reuses
        -- cached embeddings whose model still matches, so a chunking change costs no
        -- embedding calls for unchanged text.
}

-- A full pass under the current settings has reached every file: the index records
-- the settings it was built with. A pass stopped part-way (shutdown, the daily embedding
-- stop, too many consecutive rate-limit refusals, a file that could not be embedded)
-- records nothing, so the next open finds the
-- settings still differ and the full pass runs again.
rule IndexBuilt {
    when: MemoryIndexFullPassCompleted(group, settings)

    let index = MemoryIndex{group: group}

    requires: exists index

    ensures: index.built_with = settings
}

-- ---- Search ----

-- An agent queries its group's memory with a natural language string.
-- Results are ranked by hybrid vector + keyword score and filtered by min_search_score
-- (keyword-only: by keyword score, filtered by min_keyword_score).
rule MemorySearched {
    when: AgentMemorySearch(session, group, query, top_k?, path_prefix?, source?, min_score?, include_superseded?)

    requires: group = memory_group_for(session)  -- own group, or a memory provider's root requester group
    requires: group.specialist = null
    requires: group.folder in config.memory_search_groups  -- memory inactive for non-allowlisted groups
    requires: config.embedding_key_configured

    let params = MemorySearchParams{
        limit: min(top_k ?? config.search_top_k, config.search_max_top_k),
        min_score: min_score,                -- null: the default for the mode the search runs in
        path_prefix: path_prefix,
        source: source,
        include_content: null,
        include_superseded: include_superseded
    }
    let results = memory_hybrid_search(group, query, params)

    ensures: MemorySearchCompleted(group: group, query: query, results: results)

    @guidance
        -- memory_hybrid_search combines vector similarity (sqlite-vec cosine distance)
        -- and FTS5 keyword match, weighted by config.vector_score_weight and
        -- config.keyword_score_weight. Without the sqlite-vec index the vector half is an
        -- in-process cosine scan over the stored embeddings and the search stays hybrid;
        -- it runs keyword-only only when the query itself cannot be embedded (below).
        -- Returns a list of MemorySearchResult values.
        -- Results are per file: chunks of one file are grouped into one result, ranked by
        -- its best chunk, and limit counts files. Within a file, chunks whose line ranges
        -- overlap (adjacent chunks overlap by design) merge into one location keeping the
        -- better score; at most config.search_locations_per_file locations are listed,
        -- best first, and more_locations counts the rest — a term common in one file shows
        -- as one result with a count, not as the whole result list.
        -- Superseded notes (MemoryFile.superseded) are left out before ranking unless
        -- include_superseded is true: search answers with current knowledge, and history
        -- stays findable on request. A superseded note is still indexed, and still read
        -- and listed by path; the successor's supersedes and its superseded_by link them.
        -- path_prefix and source narrow the candidate set before ranking: a result inside
        -- the filter is never displaced by better-ranked results outside it, so a filtered
        -- search returns up to limit results whenever that many match.
        -- The query embedding counts against the shared (per-account, all-group) embedding
        -- budget. If no permit is
        -- granted within config.search_embed_wait (RPM/TPM cool-down, or the daily stop
        -- in force), the search runs keyword-only rather than waiting.
        -- Keyword-only results are scored by keyword match alone — weighted by
        -- config.keyword_score_weight they could never reach config.min_search_score —
        -- and an unset min_score defaults to config.min_keyword_score.
        -- If the host does not answer within config.search_host_timeout (host down,
        -- restarting, or busy), the container runs the keyword-only search itself against
        -- the read-only index mount, with the same filters. Either way the response says
        -- which mode produced it (hybrid | keyword) so the agent can tell.
        -- memory_group_for(session) is a black box (see the module header): the
        -- session's own group for a non-specialist session; for a memory-provider
        -- specialist's task session, the root requester group of its task chain,
        -- whose index it reads read-only; null for every other specialist session,
        -- whose container gets neither the memory tools nor an index mount. The host
        -- answers a search only over memory_group_for(session) - a container cannot
        -- name another group's index.
}

-- An agent retrieves the full content of a specific memory file by path.
rule MemoryFileRead {
    when: AgentMemoryGet(session, group, path, parse_frontmatter?)

    requires: group = memory_group_for(session)  -- own group, or a memory provider's root requester group
    requires: group.specialist = null
    requires: group.folder in config.memory_search_groups  -- memory inactive for non-allowlisted groups
    requires: config.embedding_key_configured
    requires: exists MemoryFile{group: group, path: path, status: indexed}

    ensures: MemoryFileContentReturned(group: group, path: path)

    @guidance
        -- Implemented by memory_get_file_content(group_folder, path, parse_frontmatter).
        -- parse_frontmatter defaults to true when absent.
        -- Same access as search: memory_group_for(session) (see MemorySearched).
}

-- An agent lists files in its group's memory workspace.
rule MemoryListed {
    when: AgentMemoryList(session, group, list_params?)

    requires: group = memory_group_for(session)  -- own group, or a memory provider's root requester group
    requires: group.specialist = null
    requires: group.folder in config.memory_search_groups  -- memory inactive for non-allowlisted groups
    requires: config.embedding_key_configured

    let params = list_params ?? MemoryListParams{
        path_prefix: null,
        source: null,
        limit: null,
        order_by: null,
        parse_frontmatter: null
    }
    let files = memory_list_files(group, params)

    ensures: MemoryListCompleted(group: group, files: files)

    @guidance
        -- Same access as search: memory_group_for(session) (see MemorySearched).
}

------------------------------------------------------------
-- Invariants
------------------------------------------------------------

-- All indexed files live either within the group's workspace — its memory/
-- subdirectory, its Zettelkasten, zettel/, or a registered workspace folder (today
-- zotero-md/), each at the top of the group workspace — or in one of the group's
-- additional mounts marked `index`, stored as extra/<mount>/…. The container config
-- that marks a mount is not modelled here, so the extra/ branch states the shape only;
-- the host indexes no other extra/ path.
-- Non-specialist agents write to memory/ and zettel/ directly (group dir is mounted RW).
-- zotero-md/ is the exception: it belongs to the Zotero sync (zotero.allium), and agents,
-- the main agent included, only read it.
-- Specialist agents land files there via commit_to_memory on result delivery
-- (group dir is mounted RO for specialists; the host performs the copy).
invariant MemoryFilesWithinWorkspace {
    for f in MemoryFiles:
        (startsWith(f.path, "groups/") and (contains(f.path, "/memory/") or contains(f.path, "/zettel/")
            or contains(f.path, "/zotero-md/")))
        or startsWith(f.path, "extra/")
}

-- A file that has never completed a sync has no chunks.
-- A file being re-indexed (status: pending, indexed_at non-null) retains its
-- stale chunks until FileSynced replaces them; this invariant permits that window.
invariant ChunksPresentOnlyWhenIndexed {
    for f in MemoryFiles:
        f.indexed_at = null implies f.chunks.count = 0
}

-- Memory indexing does not apply to specialist agent groups: nothing in a specialist
-- group's folder is watched or indexed, so no MemoryFile should ever be created for
-- one. (A memory-provider specialist reads its root requester group's index; that
-- index's files belong to that group.)
invariant NoMemoryFilesForSpecialistGroups {
    for f in MemoryFiles:
        f.group.specialist = null
}

-- Memory indexing only applies to agent groups whose folder name is in
-- config.memory_search_groups. No MemoryFile should ever be created for a group
-- not in the allowlist.
invariant NoMemoryFilesForNonAllowlistedGroups {
    for f in MemoryFiles:
        f.group.folder in config.memory_search_groups
}

------------------------------------------------------------
-- Surfaces
------------------------------------------------------------

surface MemoryMcpTools {
    facing agent: sessions/Session

    provides:
        AgentMemorySearch(agent, group, query, top_k?, path_prefix?, source?, min_score?, include_superseded?)
        AgentMemoryGet(agent, group, path, parse_frontmatter?)
        AgentMemoryList(agent, group, list_params?)

    @guidance
        -- Offered to a session exactly when memory_group_for(session) names an indexed
        -- group: every non-specialist session of an allowlisted group, and a
        -- memory-provider specialist's task session (over its root requester group's
        -- index, read-only). Other specialist sessions do not get the surface; they
        -- dispatch a memory provider as a sub-task. The group argument is filled in by
        -- the host from the session, never chosen by the agent.
}
